-- title:
--   Degree p of j(q^{p^2}) over a field containing j(qᵖ)
-- statement:
--   Work inside the Laurent series field $\mathbb{Q}((q))$, in which `jq` denotes the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant, namely $q^{-1}$ times the integral power series `jNum` with coefficients pushed into $\mathbb{Q}$, and `jqN N` denotes its image under the ring homomorphism `qExpand ℚ N` that substitutes $q \mapsto q^{N}$ (multiplication by $N$ on Hahn-series exponents), so that `jqN N` is the expansion of $j(q^{N})$. Let $F$ be an intermediate field of the extension $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$, and assume that $j(q)$ lies in $F$. Let $p$ be a natural number carrying a `Fact` instance asserting that it is prime, assume that $j(q^{p})$ lies in $F$, and assume that $j(q^{p\cdot p})$ does not lie in $F$. Then the $F$-module rank `Module.finrank` of the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $F$ generated by the single element $j(q^{p \cdot p})$ equals $p$; that is, $[F(j(q^{p^{2}})) : F] = p$ exactly.
--
--   This is the prime-square step in the algebraic determination of the degree of the modular equation: over a field already containing $j(q)$ and $j(q^{p})$, the polynomial $\Phi_p(j(q^{p}), Y)$ of degree $\psi(p) = p+1$ loses its rational root $Y = j(q)$ and the remaining degree-$p$ factor stays irreducible. It is used by [`ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_sq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.relfinrank_full_sq) in computing $[\mathbb{Q}(j, j_N) : \mathbb{Q}(j)] = \psi(N)$, the function-field description of $X_0(N)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_finrank_adjoin_jqN_sq_of_not_mem.lean

import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_X0
